---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

// Trusted static markup only (defined in this file); <code> keeps crate and API
// identifiers in monospace per the data/prose split.
const distributions = [
    {
        family: "GPUI Unofficial Mirror",
        packages: [
            {
                name: "gpui-unofficial",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-unofficial",
            },
            {
                name: "gpui-component-uo",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-component-uo",
            },
        ],
        lineage:
            "<strong>Straight from upstream Zed.</strong> <code>gpui-unofficial</code> is an automated mirror tracking bleeding-edge upstream snapshots directly (e.g., <code>1.17.0-pre</code>), paired with <code>gpui-component-uo</code> — a redistribution of Longbridge’s <code>gpui-component</code> synced up to <code>gpui-unofficial</code>.",
    },
    {
        family: "GPUI Community Edition",
        packages: [
            { name: "gpui-ce", url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-ce" },
            {
                name: "gpui_ce_components",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui_ce_components",
            },
        ],
        lineage:
            "<strong>Derivative fork with custom patches.</strong> Tailored for standalone desktop apps, paired with <code>gpui_ce_components</code> (a published fork of Longbridge’s component suite) for a pure <code>crates.io</code> dependency workflow without git overrides.",
    },
    {
        family: "GPUI Box",
        packages: [
            { name: "gpui-box", url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-box" },
            {
                name: "gpui-box-platform",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-box-platform",
            },
            {
                name: "gpui-box-kit",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/gpui-box-kit",
            },
        ],
        lineage:
            "<strong>Independent distribution cohort.</strong> A self-contained <code>0.1.x</code> package family and product-neutral component system (<code>gpui_kit</code>) for native and browser interfaces with an authoritative token model.",
    },
    {
        family: "Kael",
        packages: [
            { name: "kael", url: "https://crates.io/crates/kael" },
            { name: "kael_ui", url: "https://crates.io/crates/kael_ui" },
            {
                name: "kael_document",
                url: "https://crates.io/crates/kael_document",
            },
        ],
        lineage:
            "<strong>Fork-derived application framework.</strong> Formerly the <code>adabraka</code> fork, now published as an independent, resource-efficient GPU runtime with pre-built themeable UI components and WebAssembly support.",
    },
];
---

<Layout
    title="This Month in GPUI — August Digest"
    description="A milestone month for the GPUI ecosystem: spring animations and framerate control upstream, the first standalone crates.io distributions, Kael 0.4, gpuix, and the gpui_shell scripting runtime."
>
    <div class="page-header">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="kicker">news</p>
            <h1>This Month in GPUI — August Digest</h1>
            <p class="meta">August 2026</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    August marked a major milestone for the GPUI ecosystem.
                    Between rapid foundational updates landing in upstream Zed
                    and community initiatives delivering standalone
                    distributions, pure <code>crates.io</code> packaging, web showcases,
                    and dynamic scripting runtimes, building GPU-accelerated desktop
                    and browser interfaces in Rust is advancing quickly.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>The GPUI Archipelago &amp; Release Tracking</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                As GPUI branches out into standalone apps, web showcases, and
                specialized runtimes, community tooling has stepped in to unify
                discovery and release tracking.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        ><a href="https://github.com/gpui-archipelago"
                            >GPUI Archipelago</a
                        >:</strong
                    > A community umbrella founded on the philosophy that <em
                        >“Every fork is an island. There’s no mainland and no
                        one’s stranded.”</em
                    > It serves as a neutral hub for cross-fork discovery, toolchain
                    experiments, and shared documentation.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        ><a
                            href="https://github.com/gpui-archipelago/gpui-release-notes"
                            >gpui-release-notes</a
                        >:</strong
                    >
                    Upstream GPUI development moves rapidly within Zed’s monorepo.
                    This project isolates, tracks, and categorizes framework-specific
                    changes so downstream maintainers do not have to filter through
                    thousands of editor-specific commits.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>Upstream Highlights: v1.12.0 through v1.17.2</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                Over recent release cycles, GPUI received major upgrades across
                physics animations, rendering backends, accessibility, and
                platform integration:
            </p>

            <h3>Motion, Animations &amp; Framerate Control</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Spring Animations (v1.17.2):</strong> Physics-based simulation
                    primitives (<code>SpringConfig</code>, <code
                        >SpringState</code
                    >) modeled after SwiftUI, featuring natural momentum and
                    mid-flight interruptibility.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        >Animation &amp; Inactive Throttling (v1.17.2):</strong
                    > Added
                    <code>Animation::fps(...)</code> to cap animation refresh rates
                    on high-Hz displays, plus <code
                        >WindowOptions::inactive_frame_interval</code
                    > for background window frame throttling.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Synced Animations (v1.16.1):</strong> Introduced
                    <code>Animation::repeat_synced()</code> to lock repeating element
                    animations to a single shared phase clock.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Core Architecture &amp; Layout</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Unified <code>View</code> Trait (v1.12.0):</strong> Consolidated
                    <code>Render</code> and <code>RenderOnce</code> into a single,
                    unified <code>View</code>
                    trait across the framework.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        >External Embedding &amp; Host Loops (v1.12.0):</strong
                    > Added
                    <code>run_embedded</code> and <code>ApplicationHandle</code> to
                    drive GPUI event loops from external orchestrators.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Layout Enhancements:</strong> Container queries (<code
                        >container_query</code
                    > in v1.12.0), <code>grid_rows_min_content</code> / <code
                        >grid_rows_max_content</code
                    >
                    (v1.14.2), and Taffy CSS layout engine upgrades (0.12.2).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Axis-Locked Scrolling (v1.15.0):</strong> Fixed trackpad
                    gesture ambiguity where horizontal scrolling inadvertently triggered
                    vertical containers.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Rendering, SVG &amp; Web Backends</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>WebGL Backend (v1.15.0):</strong> Broadened web support
                    to Linux browsers where WebGPU is unavailable.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Raw SVG &amp; Multi-Scale Caching:</strong>
                    <code>svg::data(...)</code> and
                    <code>SvgSize::ExactSize</code> (v1.17.2) for raw byte rasterization,
                    alongside
                    <code>ParsedSvg</code> (v1.16.1) to avoid re-parsing SVGs during
                    continuous scaling.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Web Runtime APIs:</strong> Added async clipboard access,
                    image paste, streaming
                    <code>Fetch</code> response bodies (v1.17.2), and background worker
                    fetch requests (v1.14.2).
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Platform Integrations &amp; Tooling</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Simple Fullscreen on macOS (v1.17.2):</strong> Notch-covering
                    borderless fullscreen without creating a new Mission Control space
                    (<code>Window::toggle_simple_fullscreen()</code>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Wayland &amp; Linux Enhancements:</strong> Native Wayland
                    popups (<code>xdg_popup</code>), exclusive edge zones
                    (v1.12.0), and outbound drag-and-drop (<code
                        >wl_data_source</code
                    >, v1.15.0).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Apple Platform Groundwork (v1.17.2):</strong> Extracted
                    <code>gpui_apple</code>
                    to prepare the foundation for upcoming iOS/iPadOS runtimes alongside
                    initial touch APIs (v1.12.0).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Accessibility &amp; Diagnostics:</strong> Native accessibility
                    identifiers (<code>.accessibility_id(...)</code> in v1.16.1 for
                    AX, UIA, AT-SPI), on-screen debug histograms (v1.17.2), and deterministic
                    test settling (<code>BenchAppContext::settle()</code>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Toolchain:</strong> Baseline compiler requirement bumped
                    to
                    <code>rustc 1.97</code> (Edition 2024 ready).
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>Crates.io Packaging &amp; Distributions</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                Consuming GPUI as a standard Cargo dependency historically
                required pointing to upstream git repositories. August
                established multiple distribution options directly on
                <code>crates.io</code>:
            </p>
            <div class="table-scroll">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col">Distribution / Family</th>
                            <th scope="col">Crates.io Packages</th>
                            <th scope="col">Lineage &amp; Architecture</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        {
                            distributions.map((d) => (
                                <tr>
                                    <td class="family">{d.family}</td>
                                    <td class="packages">
                                        {d.packages.map((p) => (
                                            <a href={p.url}>{p.name}</a>
                                        ))}
                                    </td>
                                    <td class="lineage" set:html={d.lineage} />
                                </tr>
                            ))
                        }
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>Standalone Frameworks &amp; UI Kits</h2>

            <h3>GPUI Box &amp; <code>gpui_kit</code></h3>
            <p class="lede">
                Created by <a href="https://github.com/fran0220/gpui-box"
                    >fran0220</a
                >, featuring an experimental browser catalog at <a
                    href="https://gpui-box.origingame.dev/"
                    >gpui-box.origingame.dev</a
                >
                (currently in early alpha):
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Self-Contained 0.1.x Cohort:</strong> Publishes framework
                    and platform crates (<code>gpui-box</code>, <code
                        >gpui-box-platform</code
                    >, <code>gpui-box-kit</code>,
                    <code>gpui-box-wgpu</code>) with zero direct Cargo
                    dependency on Zed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Product-Neutral <code>gpui_kit</code>:</strong> Decouples
                    UI components, scene builders, and layout primitives from editor-specific
                    domain logic.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Tooling &amp; Tokens:</strong> Features centralized JSON
                    design tokens, deterministic scene test fixtures, and a Developer
                    MCP.
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Kael &amp; <code>kael_ui</code> 0.4 Release</h3>
            <p class="lede">
                The <a href="https://github.com/Augani/kael">Kael project</a> (formerly
                the
                <code>adabraka</code> GPUI fork) shipped its <strong
                    >v0.4 release</strong
                > to crates.io:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Two-Layer Architecture:</strong>
                    <code>kael</code> serves as the core GPU runtime and window manager,
                    while <code>kael_ui</code> provides an optional, themeable component
                    suite (inputs, overlays, data tables, navigation, and bundled
                    Lucide icons).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Resource-Efficient Runtime:</strong> Emphasizes zero unnecessary
                    redraws, bounded caches, and explicit capability reporting across
                    platforms.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Modular Batteries:</strong> WebView, reqwest HTTP transports,
                    and dotLottie playback are strictly opt-in feature flags.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Web &amp; Document Support:</strong> Includes <code
                        >wasm32</code
                    > browser compatibility alongside <code>kael_document</code> for
                    handling crash-recovery snapshots and session state across native
                    storage and browser persistence layers.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>GPUI &amp; Web / JS Ecosystem Bridges</h2>
            <p class="lede">
                Bridges between GPUI, the web platform, and
                JavaScript/TypeScript ecosystems are expanding quickly:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Wasm &amp; Direct Browser Compilation:</strong> Upstream
                    improvements — including WebGL fallback support, streaming <code
                        >Fetch</code
                    > bodies, and async clipboard interactions — make compiling native
                    GPUI applications directly to WebAssembly and hosting them smoothly
                    in the browser practical without custom web shims.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        ><code>gpuix</code> — React-Driven Native Elements:</strong
                    > Much like React Native, <a
                        href="https://github.com/remorses/gpuix"
                        ><code>gpuix</code></a
                    > allows developers to drive native GPUI elements directly from
                    React and TypeScript. Using
                    <code>napi-rs</code> on desktop and <code>wasm-bindgen</code
                    > on web, a custom React reconciler mutates the GPUI element tree
                    directly (avoiding JSON serialization and HTML DOM overhead) with
                    full Bun Fast Refresh support.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong
                        ><code>gpui_shell</code> — Dynamic TypeScript Control from
                        Rust:</strong
                    >
                    Longbridge’s
                    <a
                        href="https://longbridge.github.io/gpui-component/shell/#gpui-shell"
                        ><code>gpui_shell</code></a
                    >
                    integrates an embedded QuickJS runtime into native Rust applications.
                    Dynamic TypeScript plugins describe UI layouts into an arena without
                    WebViews or HTML, rendering at 120 FPS under a strict least-privilege
                    capability model (<code>gpui-shell.json</code>) with
                    auto-generated types.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>

<style>
    /* ---------- Page header ---------- */

    .page-header {
        padding-block: 64px 32px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(55, 143, 189, 0.35);
    }

    .kicker {
        margin: 0 0 12px;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 13px;
        color: var(--gray-600);
        text-transform: uppercase;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }

    .page-header h1 {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 40px;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        line-height: 1.2;
    }

    .meta {
        margin: 8px 0 0;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 13px;
        color: var(--gray-600);
    }

    /* ---------- Sections ---------- */

    .section {
        padding-block: 32px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(55, 143, 189, 0.35);
    }

    .section:last-of-type {
        border-bottom: 0;
    }

    .section h2 {
        margin: 0 0 16px;
        font-size: 32px;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        line-height: 1.25;
    }

    .section h3 {
        margin: 32px 0 12px;
        font-size: 20px;
        font-weight: 500;
        line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .lede {
        margin: 0 0 24px;
    }

    .section .container > :last-child {
        margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    .section ul {
        margin: 0 0 24px;
        padding-left: 1.4em;
    }

    .section li {
        margin-bottom: 12px;
    }

    .section li:last-child {
        margin-bottom: 0;
    }

    /* ---------- Distributions table ---------- */

    .table-scroll {
        overflow-x: auto;
    }

    table {
        width: 100%;
        min-width: 32rem;
        border-collapse: collapse;
        border: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
        border-radius: var(--radius);
        font-size: 15px;
    }

    thead th:first-child {
        border-top-left-radius: 5px;
    }

    thead th:last-child {
        border-top-right-radius: 5px;
    }

    tbody tr:last-child td:first-child {
        border-bottom-left-radius: 5px;
    }

    tbody tr:last-child td:last-child {
        border-bottom-right-radius: 5px;
    }

    th {
        background: rgba(250, 224, 183, 0.4);
        color: var(--gray-600);
        font-size: 13px;
        font-weight: 500;
        text-align: left;
        padding: 10px 16px;
    }

    td {
        padding: 12px 16px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--gray-200);
        vertical-align: top;
    }

    tbody tr:first-child td {
        border-top: 0;
    }

    .family {
        font-weight: 600;
        white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .packages a {
        display: block;
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        font-size: 14px;
        color: var(--ink);
        text-decoration: none;
    }

    .packages a + a {
        margin-top: 2px;
    }

    .packages a:hover {
        color: var(--coral);
        text-decoration: none;
    }
</style>
